  { q: 'What does the raven symbolise in Inuit culture?', a: 'In Inuit and Yupik traditions, the raven (tulugaq in Inuktitut; various forms in Yupik and related languages) is primarily a Trickster-Creator figure — a cunning, greedy, amoral being whose actions nonetheless brought important things into the world. The raven created light (or stole it and released it), helped shape the land, and taught humans important skills. The raven is simultaneously selfish and world-creative, a figure whose flaws produce beneficial outcomes. In many traditions, the raven is the ancestor of humans or a mediator between the human and spirit worlds.' },
  { q: 'Did the Inuit revere ravens?', a: 'Inuit and Yupik peoples hold complex attitudes toward the raven — combining respect, amusement, and wariness. The raven is not simply worshipped; it is engaged with as a powerful, unpredictable presence. Ravens follow hunters and can indicate the presence of game animals (particularly seals and caribou), making them practically valuable companions in a hunting economy. At the same time, the raven\'s cunning and greed make it a figure of caution — in some traditions, ravens can steal souls or deceive the unwary. The relationship is reciprocal and respectful rather than simply reverential.' },
  { q: 'Is the Inuit raven the same as the Tlingit Raven?', a: 'The Inuit-Yupik raven tradition is distinct from but related to the Tlingit-Haida and other Northwest Coast raven traditions. Both feature a Trickster-Creator raven who stole fire or light and shaped the world. The specific narratives, the raven\'s name, and the cultural context differ significantly. Inuit-Yupik traditions are circumpolar, sharing similarities with Siberian Indigenous traditions (Chukchi, Koryak) where the raven is similarly a Trickster-Creator. The Northwest Coast traditions (Tlingit, Haida, Tsimshian) are more elaborate and ceremonially central but share the same fundamental Trickster-Creator archetype.' },
];
